.TH ADRCTL 1 "2026-09-06" "adrctl" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
adrctl \- create, link, list, and report on Architecture Decision Records
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B adrctl
.RI [ --project-root " PATH" ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTION ...]
.RI [ ARGUMENT ...]
.PP
.B adrctl
.RB { -h | --help | --version }
.PP
.B adrctl help
.RI [ COMMAND
.RI [ SUBCOMMAND ...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B adrctl
is a Bash command-line tool for managing Architecture Decision Records (ADRs).
It is a behavior-compatible successor to
.B adr-tools
where practical, while adding stricter configuration handling, safer mutation
preflight, configurable rendering, and additional report formats.
.PP
The canonical command name is
.BR adrctl .
The same executable may also be invoked through a filesystem symbolic link named
.B adr
for compatibility with established
.B adr-tools
workflows.  This compatibility name does not select a separate runtime mode.
.PP
Commands that operate on ADR files discover a project root and ADR directory,
then work with the managed ADR set selected by the effective project
configuration.  Normal runtime operation does not require network access.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS init
.B adrctl init
.RI [ DIRECTORY ]
.PP
Initialize an ADR repository and create the first ADR without opening an editor.
If
.I DIRECTORY
is omitted, the ADR directory defaults to
.B doc/adr
relative to the project root.  If a directory is supplied, it is created as
needed and recorded in
.B .adr-dir
when appropriate.
.PP
Initialization fails rather than silently reinitializing an ADR directory that
already contains managed ADRs.  On success, the created ADR pathname is written
to standard output.
.SS new
.B adrctl new
.RB [ -s " REFERENCE" ]...
.RB [ -l " TARGET:LINK:REVERSE-LINK" ]...
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I TITLE...
.PP
Create the next numbered ADR.  Title arguments are joined with single spaces.
The default filename pattern is
.BR {NUMBER4}-{TITLE_SLUG}.md ,
and the default status for an ordinary new ADR is
.BR Accepted .
.PP
The following options are supported:
.TP
.BI -s " REFERENCE"
Supersede an existing ADR.  This option may be repeated.  The new ADR receives a
.B Supercedes
relationship and the target receives the reciprocal
.B Superceded by
relationship, preserving the historical observable spelling.
.TP
.BI -l " TARGET:LINK:REVERSE-LINK"
Add a reciprocal relationship to an existing ADR.  This option may be repeated.
The value must contain exactly three non-empty colon-delimited fields.
.TP
.BI --template " PATH"
Use an explicit ADR body template.
.TP
.BI --filename-pattern " PATTERN"
Override the filename pattern for this creation operation.
.TP
.BI --start-delimiter " STRING"
Set the ADR body-template starting delimiter.
.TP
.BI --end-delimiter " STRING"
Set the ADR body-template ending delimiter.  Starting and ending delimiter
options must be supplied together at the same configuration layer.
.PP
After a new ADR and any reciprocal relationship changes are written,
.B adrctl
opens the created ADR using
.B VISUAL
or
.BR EDITOR ,
in that order.  If neither is configured, editing is skipped.  On success, the
created ADR pathname is written to standard output.
.SS link
.B adrctl link
.I SOURCE LINK TARGET REVERSE-LINK
.PP
Add a relationship from
.I SOURCE
to
.I TARGET
and a reciprocal relationship from
.I TARGET
to
.IR SOURCE .
Both ADR references and both required Status sections are validated before file
replacement begins.
.SS list
.B adrctl list
.PP
Write one managed ADR pathname per line to standard output in logical numeric
order.  Paths are presented relative to the caller's current working directory
when possible.
.SS generate
.B adrctl generate
.RI [ REPORT
.RI [ OPTION ...]]
.PP
With no report name,
.B adrctl
writes the available report names in deterministic order:
.B toc
and
.BR graph .
.SS "generate toc"
.B adrctl generate toc
.RB [ -i " INTRO_FILE" ]
.RB [ -o " OUTRO_FILE" ]
.RB [ -p " LINK_PREFIX" ]
.PP
Generate a Markdown table of contents on standard output.  The report begins with
.BR "# Architecture Decision Records" .
An optional intro file is inserted before ADR entries and an optional outro file
is appended after them.
.TP
.BI -i " INTRO_FILE"
Insert readable file content after the report heading and before ADR entries.
.TP
.BI -o " OUTRO_FILE"
Append readable file content after ADR entries.
.TP
.BI -p " LINK_PREFIX"
Prefix generated ADR links with the supplied string.
.SS "generate graph"
.B adrctl generate graph
.RB [ -p " LINK_PREFIX" ]
.RB [ -e " LINK_EXTENSION" ]
.RB [ --format " dot|mermaid" ]
.PP
Generate the ADR relationship graph as textual graph source on standard output.
Graph construction is shared across serializers; only the textual representation
changes.
.TP
.BI -p " LINK_PREFIX"
Prefix generated node links with the supplied string.
.TP
.BI -e " LINK_EXTENSION"
Replace the default link extension,
.BR .html ,
with the supplied extension.
.TP
.BI --format " dot|mermaid"
Select the graph serialization.  The default is
.BR dot .
.B mermaid
emits raw Mermaid flowchart source without Markdown code fences.
.PP
Generating DOT does not require Graphviz.  Generating Mermaid does not require
Mermaid CLI, Node.js, a browser, or another Mermaid renderer.
.SS upgrade-repository
.B adrctl upgrade-repository
.PP
Upgrade recognized legacy ADR data.  The current migration converts managed ADR
Date lines of the form
.B Date: DD/MM/YYYY
to
.BR "Date: YYYY-MM-DD" .
Other lines are preserved, and already-converted dates are unchanged by a second
run.
.SS help
.B adrctl help
.RI [ COMMAND
.RI [ SUBCOMMAND ...]]
.PP
Display built-in help for the requested command path.  Help is shown through a
pager selected from
.BR ADR_PAGER ,
.BR PAGER ,
and
.B more
in that order.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --project-root " PATH"
Explicitly select the project root for commands that use project state.  This
option must appear before the command name and may be supplied at most once.
.TP
.BR -h , " " --help
Display top-level help.
.TP
.B --version
Display immutable build metadata using a stable three-line form containing the
product version, source-revision build date, and source commit.
.SH PROJECT DISCOVERY
For commands that operate on an existing project, the project root is selected in
this order:
.IP 1. 3
.B --project-root
.IR PATH .
.IP 2. 3
The process environment variable
.BR ADRCTL_PROJECT_ROOT .
.IP 3. 3
The nearest recognized ancestor marker while walking upward from the current
working directory.
.IP 4. 3
The Git work-tree root, when Git can determine one.
.IP 5. 3
The current working directory.
.PP
Recognized ancestor markers are:
.IP \(bu 2
A
.B .adr-dir
file.
.IP \(bu 2
An existing
.B doc/adr
directory.
.IP \(bu 2
A
.B .env
file containing at least one syntactically valid assignment whose key begins
with
.BR ADRCTL_ .
.PP
A generic
.B .env
file containing no
.B ADRCTL_
assignment does not establish ADR project context.
.PP
.B init
is an exception: unless explicitly redirected with
.B --project-root
or
.BR ADRCTL_PROJECT_ROOT ,
it initializes the current working directory rather than climbing to a Git root.
.SH CONFIGURATION
After selecting the project root,
.B adrctl
reads
.B .env
at that root when present.  The file is parsed as inert data; it is not sourced,
evaluated, or shell-expanded.
.PP
Non-
.B ADRCTL_
keys are ignored.  Unknown
.B ADRCTL_
project keys are configuration errors.
.PP
Project-scoped settings follow this precedence from highest to lowest:
.IP 1. 3
Command-line option, where one exists.
.IP 2. 3
Process environment.
.IP 3. 3
Project
.BR .env .
.IP 4. 3
Compatible legacy project metadata, such as
.BR .adr-dir ,
where applicable.
.IP 5. 3
Built-in default.
.PP
Supported project
.B ADRCTL_
keys include:
.TP
.B ADRCTL_ADR_DIR
Select the ADR directory.
.TP
.B ADRCTL_ADR_GLOB
Select candidate basenames within the ADR directory.  The default is
.BR *.md .
The value is basename-scoped and may not contain
.BR / .
.TP
.B ADRCTL_ADR_NUMBER_REGEX
Recognize managed ADR basenames and capture the complete logical decimal ADR
number in regular-expression capture group 1.  The default recognizes common
forms such as
.BR 0001-example.md ,
.BR ADR-0001-example.md ,
and
.BR decision-0001-example.md .
.TP
.B ADRCTL_TEMPLATE
Select the ADR body template.
.TP
.B ADRCTL_FILENAME_PATTERN
Select the filename pattern for newly created ADRs.
.TP
.B ADRCTL_TEMPLATE_START_DELIMITER
Set the body-template starting delimiter.
.TP
.B ADRCTL_TEMPLATE_END_DELIMITER
Set the body-template ending delimiter.
.PP
.B ADRCTL_PROJECT_ROOT
is valid as a process-environment or command-line concept only.  Placing it in a
project
.B .env
file is a configuration error.
.SH ADR DIRECTORY AND DISCOVERY
The ADR directory is selected from the effective configuration, then compatible
.B .adr-dir
metadata, and finally defaults to
.B doc/adr
relative to the project root.
.PP
Managed ADR discovery uses one shared pipeline: candidate basenames are selected
with
.BR ADRCTL_ADR_GLOB ,
then validated with
.BR ADRCTL_ADR_NUMBER_REGEX .
All collection-driven operations, including listing, reference resolution,
next-number allocation, TOC generation, graph generation, and repository upgrades,
use this same managed ADR set.
.PP
Newly rendered filenames are validated against the effective discovery contract
before publication so
.B adrctl
does not knowingly create an ADR that later commands would ignore or assign a
different logical number.
.SH ADR REFERENCES
Commands accepting an ADR reference support:
.IP \(bu 2
An exact logical decimal ADR number.
.IP \(bu 2
An exact filename or basename.
.IP \(bu 2
A unique partial filename fragment.
.PP
Exact filename matches take precedence over partial matches.  Numeric references
match the complete logical number and ignore presentation zero padding.  No match
or an ambiguous match is an operational failure; ambiguous references are not
resolved by selecting an incidental first match.
.SH TEMPLATES
ADR body templates are selected in this order:
.IP 1. 3
.B --template
.IR PATH .
.IP 2. 3
.BR ADRCTL_TEMPLATE .
.IP 3. 3
Legacy
.BR ADR_TEMPLATE .
.IP 4. 3
Project
.BR ADRCTL_TEMPLATE .
.IP 5. 3
.B templates/template.md
beneath the effective ADR directory, when readable.
.IP 6. 3
The built-in template.
.PP
The render context includes values such as
.BR NUMBER ,
.BR NUMBER4 ,
.BR TITLE ,
.BR TITLE_SLUG ,
.BR STATUS ,
.BR DATE ,
.BR PROJECT_ROOT ,
and
.BR ADR_DIR .
.PP
When no explicit delimiter pair is configured, body-template syntax is detected
conservatively.  A recognized braced context token selects
.B {
and
.B }
as delimiters; otherwise empty delimiters select legacy bare-token mode.  One
body render uses exactly one delimiter pair.
.PP
Filename patterns are independent of body-template delimiter settings and always
use the braced
.B {KEY}
grammar.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ADRCTL_PROJECT_ROOT
Override project-root discovery for the current process.
.TP
.B ADRCTL_ADR_DIR
Override the ADR directory.
.TP
.B ADRCTL_ADR_GLOB
Override candidate-basename selection.
.TP
.B ADRCTL_ADR_NUMBER_REGEX
Override managed-ADR number recognition.
.TP
.B ADRCTL_TEMPLATE
Override the body-template path.
.TP
.B ADRCTL_FILENAME_PATTERN
Override the filename pattern.
.TP
.B ADRCTL_TEMPLATE_START_DELIMITER
Set the body-template starting delimiter.
.TP
.B ADRCTL_TEMPLATE_END_DELIMITER
Set the body-template ending delimiter.
.TP
.B ADR_TEMPLATE
Legacy body-template override retained for compatibility.
.TP
.B ADR_DATE
Override the date used for newly created ADRs.
.TP
.B VISUAL
Preferred editor for a newly created ADR.
.TP
.B EDITOR
Fallback editor when
.B VISUAL
is unset or empty.
.TP
.B ADR_PAGER
Preferred pager for help output.
.TP
.B PAGER
Fallback pager when
.B ADR_PAGER
is unset or empty.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B .env
Project configuration file at the resolved project root.
.TP
.B .adr-dir
Compatible legacy ADR-directory selector at the project root.
.TP
.B doc/adr/
Default ADR directory relative to the project root.
.TP
.B doc/adr/templates/template.md
Default project-local ADR body template when readable and no higher-precedence
template is selected.
.SH STANDARD STREAMS
Standard output is reserved for requested results or explicit informational
output, including created ADR pathnames, ADR listings, generated reports, help,
and version information.
.PP
Diagnostics, warnings, configuration errors, migration notices, and failure
explanations are written to standard error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
Operational or domain failure, such as an unresolved or ambiguous ADR reference,
unreadable required file, detected destination collision, or failing external
editor or pager.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command usage or invalid
.B adrctl
configuration, such as an unknown option, malformed argument, unknown
.B ADRCTL_
project key, invalid delimiter pair, invalid discovery configuration, or
unsupported graph format.
.PP
A process terminated by a signal may retain Bash's normal signal-derived process
status.
.SH EXAMPLES
Initialize the default
.B doc/adr
directory:
.PP
.nf
.RS
adrctl init
.RE
.fi
.PP
Create a new ADR:
.PP
.nf
.RS
adrctl new "Use PostgreSQL for durable application state"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Create a new ADR that supersedes ADR 12:
.PP
.nf
.RS
adrctl new -s 12 "Replace the legacy persistence design"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Add reciprocal relationships between two ADRs:
.PP
.nf
.RS
adrctl link 12 "Depends on" 7 "Required by"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Generate a Markdown table of contents:
.PP
.nf
.RS
adrctl generate toc > adr-index.md
.RE
.fi
.PP
Generate Graphviz DOT:
.PP
.nf
.RS
adrctl generate graph > adr-relationships.dot
.RE
.fi
.PP
Generate raw Mermaid source:
.PP
.nf
.RS
adrctl generate graph --format mermaid > adr-relationships.mmd
.RE
.fi
.PP
Operate on a project explicitly rather than using discovery:
.PP
.nf
.RS
adrctl --project-root /path/to/project list
.RE
.fi
.SH COMPATIBILITY
.B adrctl
uses
.B adr-tools
3.0.0 as the canonical predecessor compatibility baseline.  Supported predecessor
workflows are preserved where practical, while documented safety deviations may
fail earlier or more conservatively.
.PP
Notable intentional differences include rejection of ambiguous ADR references,
strict parsing of project configuration as data, rejection of malformed
.B -l
relationship specifications with extra fields, and the absence of external
.B adr-*
or
.B adrctl-*
plugin discovery.
.PP
The canonical product identity remains
.BR adrctl .
A filesystem symbolic link named
.B adr
may point to the same executable for historical command-name compatibility.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bash (1),
.BR git (1),
.BR man (1)
.PP
The normative behavioral specification is maintained in
.B doc/adrctl-spec.md
in the adrctl source repository.  Architecture decisions are maintained under
.BR doc/adr/ .
